course: world
lesson: 56
title: Energy — where it comes from and how to save it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Energy is what makes things happen: it moves cars, warms houses, lights rooms and lets us run and think. Energy is never made from nothing; it only changes from one form to another.
explain: Almost all our energy starts in the Sun. Plants catch sunlight to make food; we eat plants, or animals that ate plants, and get energy for our bodies.
explain: Coal, oil and gas are fuels made from plants and tiny sea creatures that lived millions of years ago. Burning them releases energy but also smoke and carbon dioxide, and once used they are gone — they are non-renewable.
explain: Sunlight, wind, flowing water and the heat inside the Earth will not run out — they are renewable. Solar panels turn sunlight into electricity, and wind turbines turn the moving air into electricity.
explain: Saving energy means wasting less: switching off lights and screens, closing doors to keep heat in, walking or cycling instead of driving short distances.
fact: Energy is needed to make anything move, heat up, light up or grow.
fact: Energy changes from one form into another, but it is never made from nothing.
fact: Most of the energy on Earth comes from the Sun.
fact: Food gives our bodies energy, and that energy came from the Sun through plants.
fact: Coal, oil and natural gas are fossil fuels formed from living things millions of years ago.
fact: Fossil fuels are non-renewable because they take millions of years to form.
fact: Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, which warms the Earth's climate.
fact: Sun, wind, flowing water and heat from inside the Earth are renewable energy sources.
fact: A solar panel changes sunlight into electricity.
fact: A wind turbine changes the movement of the wind into electricity.
fact: A battery stores energy in chemicals.
fact: Switching off things we are not using saves energy.
exercise: What is energy? | What makes things move, heat up, light up or change | A kind of metal used in batteries | A gas found only in the air | Something only machines have
exercise: Where does most of the energy on Earth come from? | The Sun | The Moon | The Earth's oceans | Power stations
exercise: Where did the energy in your breakfast cereal first come from? | The Sun, caught by the plants | The factory that made the box | The milk you poured on it | The electricity in the kitchen
exercise: What are coal, oil and gas made from? | Remains of living things from millions of years ago | Rocks melted by volcanoes last century | Seawater dried by the Sun | Metals buried deep in the ground
exercise: Why are fossil fuels called non-renewable? | They take millions of years to form | They can never be burned, however hot it gets | They make no smoke at all when burned | They grow back every year, like trees do
exercise: Which of these is a renewable source of energy? | The wind | Coal | Oil | Natural gas
exercise: What does a solar panel do? | Changes sunlight into electricity | Changes electricity into sunlight | Stores coal for winter | Makes the wind blow
exercise: What does a wind turbine use to make electricity? | The movement of the air | The heat of burning wood | The light of the Moon | Water from the clouds only
exercise: What gas released by burning fossil fuels warms the Earth's climate? | Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Helium | Nitrogen
exercise: Which of these saves energy at home? | Switching off lights in empty rooms | Leaving the TV on standby all night | Opening windows while the heating is on | Leaving the fridge door open
exercise: When you ride a bicycle, where does the energy to move come from? | The food you have eaten | The air in the tyres | The metal of the frame | The friction with the road
exercise: What does a battery store? | Energy in chemicals | Light in a tiny bulb | Wind for later | Heat from the Sun only
exercise: Which is true about energy? | It changes form but is not made from nothing | It can be made from nothing by clever machines | It disappears forever when used | It exists only in electricity
test: A town builds a dam on a river to make electricity. What kind of energy source is it using? | A renewable one, flowing water | A non-renewable one, like coal | A fossil fuel from the riverbed | Energy made from nothing
test: A family wants to use less energy. Which plan helps most? | Walk to the nearby shop and switch off unused lights | Drive to the nearby shop every day | Leave chargers and screens on all night | Keep the heating on with windows open
test: Why will the world eventually run short of oil but not of sunshine? | Oil forms far too slowly, but the Sun keeps shining | Oil is all used up already | Sunshine is stored underground | The Sun is made of oil
test: A lamb grows by eating grass. Where did the energy in the lamb first come from? | The Sun, through the grass | The soil the grass grew in | The rain on the field | The farmer's tractor
test: Which is true about a wind turbine? | It turns the movement of wind into electricity | It makes the wind blow harder | It burns fuel to spin | It works only when there is no wind blowing at all
test: When coal is burned in a power station, what happens to its energy? | It changes into heat and then electricity | It disappears completely into the air for ever | It turns back into living plants in the soil | It is made bigger by the fire in the boiler
test: Why do many countries build more solar panels and wind turbines? | They are renewable and do not release carbon dioxide while working | They work without sunlight or wind | They are made from oil and gas | They make the weather warmer
test: A toy car runs on a battery until it stops. What has happened? | The chemical energy in the battery has been used | The car has become too heavy | The wheels have stored up all of the energy inside them | The battery has turned into coal
test: where'd the energy in my cereal come from | From the Sun, through the plants | From the milk carton in the fridge | From the big machines at the factory | From the bowl it was poured into
test: whats a solar panel actually do | It changes sunlight into electricity | It stores coal for later, like a battery | It makes the sun brighter | It turns wind into heat
test: my friend says a battery makes energy out of nothing. true? | No, it stores chemical energy | Yes, batteries make new energy | Yes, that is why they never run out | No, batteries store only heat inside
test: a video said burning coal is clean and doesn't change the climate. right? | No, it releases carbon dioxide which warms the climate | Yes, coal smoke is clean | Yes, coal cools the Earth | No, coal releases only water
exam: Ascertain the principal source of the greater part of the energy available upon the Earth. | The Sun | The Moon | The Earth's oceans | The Earth's magnetism
exam: Identify the principal reason for which coal, oil and natural gas are designated non-renewable. | Their formation requires millions of years | They are wholly incapable of combustion in any furnace | They are replenished upon each day | They are derived from sunlight directly
exam: Select, from the sources hereinafter enumerated, that which is designated a renewable source of energy. | The wind | Coal | Petroleum | Natural gas
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the nature of energy? | It is converted from one form into another but is never created from nothing | It is created from nothing whenever a machine is operated | It is destroyed entirely upon each use | It exists solely in living organisms
